Energy · October 2, 2026
G7 will release 100 million barrels over four months, counting commitments already fulfilled
G7 calls a diesel release substantial and places it in the first 20 days, with no number. A French presidency readout says up to 100 million barrels of diesel and crude.
100 million barrels coordinated release beginning immediately
4 months the period named for that release
20 days window for the frontloaded diesel release
400 million barrels March coordinated release
What happened
On October 2, 2026, G7 leaders said that, "taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days". The same statement says the group will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns. It reaffirms a commitment to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries.
The French presidency's readout states a ceiling, "jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois". That readout names refined products, notably diesel and crude.
The March contributions page says the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products. A barrel counted as crude is not the diesel a pump sells. On September 30, Energy Secretary Chris Wright said diesel supplies are "tight" because of export loss related to conflicts in Russia and the Middle East. Chris Wright said American refiners are running at record highs.
The October 2 statement calls for the immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz. The French readout says volumes moving through Hormuz and the Yanbu canal in the Red Sea have been increasing in recent days. Notably, net exports of diesel/gasoil from the Gulf countries averaged 390 kb/d in August, just over a quarter of levels before the war in the Middle East. Combined, net exports of diesel/gasoil exports from the Gulf and Russia in August were 1.6 mb/d lower than in February.
On March 11 the Energy Department said 32 member nations of the International Energy Agency agreed to a coordinated release of 400 million barrels. The United States would release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, beginning next week. On September 29 the department requested an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil. It said earlier solicitations collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels. It said several European member countries have released only a fraction. On September 30 President Donald Trump was still "thinking about" banning diesel exports. On October 2 a Reuters brief said President Donald Trump will not authorize an export ban on diesel. The same day's statement reaffirms a commitment to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries. The March contributions page says this is the sixth time that IEA Member countries have taken emergency collective action. Previous collective actions were taken in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022.
The catch
A massive immediate release of Europe's diesel
President Donald Trump posted that Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil and that the process will begin immediately. The joint statement counts commitments already fulfilled and calls for a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days.
Donald Trump · October 2, 2026 · Truth Social, carried by Trump's Truth
Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately.
Trump's Truth
100 million barrels of diesel in 20 days, or 50 and 50
Reuters reported a demand to "release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window". A Friday proposal, before the statement, said European countries would release 50 million barrels of diesel and International Energy Agency members would release 50 million barrels of crude oil.
The day before, Reuters reported: "A second source, based in a European capital, said that the US has asked the EU to release 120 million barrels of diesel in the next six months."
An X post said that "100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States".
The joint statement counts commitments already fulfilled and states a coordinated release of 100 million barrels over 4 months.
100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel
The Wall Street Journal's lede said the countries would "release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks". IEA Executive Director Fatih Birol noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action. The March contributions sheet's total line is Total IEA,426.03075080054771.
Where this sits
The Energy Department said International Energy Agency member nations agreed a coordinated release of 400 million barrels, and the October 2 text sets a timetable of 4 months.
